{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q018_tal_effector_repeat_lengths","answer":"All three domains sit in SCOPe 2.08 (the current release, per /api/v1/ and /api/v1/releases/current) under family a.298.1.1, TAL (transcription activator-like) effector. Its parent is superfamily a.298.1 (TAL effector-like, sunid 267596), which sits under a.298. Family membership is not a length-based grouping. SCOPe places domains by shared structural architecture and common evolutionary origin of the structural unit. In TAL effectors that unit is a tandem array of roughly 33-35-residue repeat modules, each forming a helix-loop-helix that stacks into a superhelix (pretrained knowledge: Boch et al. 2009; Moscou & Bogdanove 2009). Total length mostly reflects how many repeat copies a protein has plus its flanking non-repeat regions, and these vary independently of the repeat fold.

What the API data shows: d2kq5a_ (PthA, Xanthomonas axonopodis pv. citri) has a 51-residue SEQRES record, about one and a half repeats, and is an NMR-scale fragment. d3v6pa_ (dHax3, Xanthomonas) has a 373-residue record, about 11 repeats at ~34 residues each. d4cj9a_ (BurrH, Burkholderia rhizoxinica) has a 752-residue record, about 22 repeats. All three contain the same repeat signature (for example ...LTPEQVVAIAS..GGKQALETVQRLLPVL...), which is the shared element that drives the family call. The repeat counts are my estimates from the ~34-residue repeat length, not values the API returned.

Limits: (1) Shared family membership reflects a structural and evolutionary grouping of the repeat module. It does not show that the three proteins have the same DNA target, that they are orthologs, or that their functions are interchangeable. The hypervariable residues in each repeat determine DNA-base specificity and differ between proteins, so do not transfer target specificity or function between them on the basis of the family call. (2) Do not use the 51-residue d2kq5a_ fragment as a template for full-length TAL effector modelling. Choose templates by repeat count and full-length context. (3) I did not verify SCOPe domain range boundaries. The sequences endpoint returned chain SEQRES records (source: seqres), so the 51, 373, and 752 values are sequence record lengths, not confirmed domain-range lengths. (4) To claim binding specificity or functional equivalence, external evidence is required beyond SCOPe, such as repeat-DNA complex structures or DNA-binding assays with the repeat-variable residue composition.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge (TAL effector repeat architecture)","/api/v1/","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d2kq5a_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d3v6pa_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/resolve/d4cj9a_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/264989","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/265556","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/265996","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267704","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267705","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267706","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267645","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267646","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267647","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267609","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/267596","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d2kq5a_?release=2.08","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d3v6pa_?release=2.08","/api/v1/sequences/sids/d4cj9a_?release=2.08"]}]}
